-- title:
--   Function field embedding into the Čerednik–Drinfeld model over C
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $N$ is a nonzero squarefree natural number, $q$ and $q'$ are primes with $q' \ne q$, neither dividing $N$, both at least $5$, and $D$ is a nonzero natural number divisible by $2Nqq'$. Write $C =$ `A₁.valuation.Completion` for the completion of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ at a valuation subring $A_1 \subseteq \overline{\mathbb Q}$ with `A₁.LiesOverPrime q'`, i.e. $q'$ lies in the nonunits of $A_1$, and write $C_0 =$ [`ValuationSubring.ratClosure A₁`](def/ValuationSubring_CompletionRatClosure.html#L13) for the topological closure of the prime subfield inside $C$.
--
--   The hypotheses fall into the following groups.
--
--   *Definite quaternionic data at $q$* (the index-$2$ data). $a_2, b_2 \in \mathbb Q$ satisfy `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt q`: $a_2 < 0$, $b_2 < 0$, and a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ is ramified for $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a_2, b_2]$ (every nonzero element of $\mathbb H \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a unit) exactly when $q \in v$. Submodules $\Lambda_2, R_2 \subseteq \mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a_2, b_2]$ are given with $\Lambda_2$ a maximal order, $R_2$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$ in the first), and $R_2 \le \Lambda_2$. A finite idele $n_2$ lies in `primeHeckeSet R₂ q'`; `meetOrder R₂ n₂` $= R_2 \cap R_2^{n_2}$ is an Eichler order of level $Nq'$ (`hS₂`); $n_2$ normalises it (`hnorm₂`); the shift by $n_2$ on the class set of the stabiliser of `meetOrder R₂ n₂` is an involution (`hsq₂`); and `hlaws₂`, the predicate `ClassSetHeckeLaws N q' Λ₂ R₂ n₂`, asserts commutativity of the edge Hecke matrices, commutativity of the vertex Hecke matrices, compatibility of the degeneracy maps $\delta_i$ with the Hecke matrices away from $q'$, and stability of the joint kernel of the $\delta_i$ under the edge Hecke operators.
--
--   *Indefinite quaternionic data and the moduli problem.* $a, b \in \mathbb Q$ satisfy `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q q'`: $a > 0$ or $b > 0$, and the finite ramified places are exactly those containing $q$ or $q'$; $\Lambda \subseteq \mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a, b]$ is a maximal order. $\bar F$ is a field, of essentially finite type over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and a curve over it in the sense of `IsCurveOver` (principal divisors exist and have degree $0$, residue fields of places are finite over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and $\Omega_{\bar F/\overline{\mathbb Q}}$ is free of rank one). A scheme $X$ with a morphism $\pi_X$ to $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $\mathbb Z[1/D]$, a point $\bar s$ of that base over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and a family $pt$ assigning to each ring $S$, each $S$-point $s$ of the base and each fake elliptic curve $E$ over $S$ with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure a morphism over $s$ to $X$; the hypotheses `pt_iso`, `pt_pullback`, `pt_surjective`, `pt_injective` state exactly the four non-universal clauses of `IsCoarseModuli` for these data: invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change along ring maps and pullback of curves, and bijectivity on isomorphism classes over algebraically closed fields.
--
--   *The geometric model and the tower.* $\mathfrak M$ is a `CurveModel` for $\bar F$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (an integral proper smooth relative-dimension-one curve with an identification of $\bar F$ with its function field, compatible with the base, and a bijection of closed points with places satisfying the stalk condition), $e_{\mathfrak M}$ is an isomorphism of $\mathfrak M.C$ with the pullback of $\pi_X$ along $\bar s$ commuting with the structure maps (`he𝔐`), $gal$ is a homomorphism from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ to the semilinear automorphisms of $\bar F/\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $\mathbb T$ is a `HeckeTower.TowerData q q' Fbar` (fields $\mathbb T.F(\ell)$ for primes $\ell \ne q, q'$, curves over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, with finite integral maps $\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell, k)$ from $\bar F$ for $k \in \{0,1\}$), $galT$ gives the corresponding Galois actions on the $\mathbb T.F(\ell)$, and $W : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to$ `SemilinearAut`, $WT$ likewise for each $\ell$. The witness $tw$ of type `ModuliTowerWitnessD Λ N q q' D Fbar X πX sbar pt 𝔐 e𝔐 gal 𝕋 galT W WT` packages the moduli interpretation of the tower: representing fake elliptic curves for places of $\bar F$ and of the $\mathbb T.F(\ell)$, compatibility of $pt$ with the point–place bijection, bijectivity of the level-$\ell$ representation up to isomorphism, the prescriptions that $gal$ and $galT$ induce the given Galois action on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ while $W$ and $WT$ act trivially on it, and the identification of the two degeneracy maps and of the $W$'s with level restriction, level isogeny and Atkin–Lehner quotients.
--
--   *The place $q'$ and the $p$-adic uniformisation data.* `hiso₁` states that the decomposition group of $A_1$ over $\mathbb Q$ acts isometrically for the valuation; $v_1$ is a height-one prime of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ containing $q'$. An injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_1 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a_2, b_2] \to M_2(C_0)$ is given, together with the induced projective representation $\rho_1$ on units (`hρ₁`), a pseudo-uniformiser $\varpi_1$ of $C_0$ in $C$ whose image is $q'$ (`hϖ₁`), and the holomorphic ring `HolRingOf ϖ₁ ρ₁` is assumed to be a domain.
--
--   *Hecke elements, congruence subgroups and Atkin–Lehner elements in the definite algebra.* Families $s_1(\ell) \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q, a_2, b_2]^\times$ and $sf_1(\ell)$ in the finite-adelic units are given such that, for every prime $\ell \ne q, q'$, the local components of $sf_1(\ell)$ away from $q'$ are $s_1(\ell) \otimes 1$, its component at $q'$ is $1$, the product of the diagonal idele of $\ell$ with $sf_1(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₂ (meetOrder R₂ n₂) ℓ` if $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₂ n₂) ℓ` otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_1(\ell)) = \ell$. A family of subgroups $\Gamma_1$ indexed by `HeckeTower.Obj q q'` is specified by: $\Gamma_1(\mathrm{none})$ consists of the units of `awayUnits R₂ v₁` whose reduced norm has even $q'$-adic valuation (`hΓ₁0`), and $\Gamma_1(\mathrm{some}\,\ell)$ is the intersection of $\Gamma_1(\mathrm{none})$ with its conjugate by $s_1(\ell)$ (`hΓ₁ℓ`). Elements $w_1$ and $\bar w_1$, again indexed by `HeckeTower.Obj q q'`, satisfy `hw₁` (reduced norm $q'$ and membership in the relevant away-units subgroup, for the base index with respect to $R_2$ and for $\ell$ with respect to `meetOrder R₂ (sf₁ ℓ)`) and `hwbar₁` (reduced norm $q$, local integrality away from $v_1$ at places not containing $q$, and normalisation of the local boxes of the relevant order and of $\Lambda_2$ at every place $u \ne v_1$, stated both for the base index and for each $\ell$).
--
--   *Local data at $q'$.* A homomorphism $dIso_1$ from the decomposition group of $A_1$ to the isometric automorphisms of $C$ over $C_0$, realising the Galois action (`hdIso₁`); a quadratic character $\chi$ of the decomposition group trivial on inertia (`hχ₁`), nontrivial on any Frobenius at $q'$ (`hχ₂`), and with kernel characterised by fixing the residue field elements satisfying $x^{q'^2} = x$ (`hχ₃`).
--
--   *The integral model.* $\mathcal O_0$ is a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero with uniformiser $\pi$, residue cardinality $q'$ and $(q') = (\pi)$ (so absolutely unramified over $\mathbb Z_{q'}$), with $C_0$ as its fraction field; $Onr$ is a domain over $\mathcal O_0$ with a lift of Frobenius $Fr$, $\pi$-adically complete, with $(\pi)$ maximal, integral and henselian-style surjectivity on monic polynomials (`hOa`, `hOcl`), and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{q'}$ modulo $\pi$ (`hFr`). A determinant valuation $vdet$ on $\mathrm{GL}_2(C_0)$ is normalised by `hvdet`; $\psi_0 : Onr \to C$ is a ring map with image of valuation at most $1$, compatible with $\mathcal O_0 \to C_0 \to C$ (`hψ₀𝒪`); $\pi$ corresponds to $\varpi_1$ (`hπϖ`), $N$ is a unit in $\mathcal O_0$, the elements of $C_0$ of valuation at most $1$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O_0$ (`h𝒪₀`), and `hrk` is a rank-one (Archimedean value group) property of the valuation of $C$.
--
--   *Moduli over $\mathcal O_0$, degeneracies and Atkin–Lehner maps.* $f_0 : \mathcal X_0 \to \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal O_0$ with the family $pt_0$ is a coarse moduli space for fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level $N$ in the full sense of `IsCoarseModuli` (`h𝒳₀`), with $\mathcal X_0$ integral and $f_0$ flat, separated, locally of finite type and quasi-compact; for each prime $\ell \ne q', q$, $g_0(\ell) : \mathcal Y_0(\ell) \to \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal O_0$ with $ptT_0(\ell)$ is a coarse moduli space for the same objects with extra level-$\ell$ structure (`h𝒴₀`); $d_{0}(\ell), d_{1}(\ell) : \mathcal Y_0(\ell) \to \mathcal X_0$ are morphisms over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal O_0$ (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) computing on points the forgetful map (`hd₀`) and the level-$\ell$ isogeny quotient (`hd₁`); $ar, \overline{ar} : \mathcal X_0 \to \mathcal X_0$ are morphisms over the base (`harf`, `harbarf`) realising the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $q'$ and at $q$ respectively on points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   *The Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation datum `hCD`.* Families $\Theta$ and $\Theta T(\ell)$ assign, to every $\mathcal O_0$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map from the points over $B$ of the product of the functor corepresented by $Onr$ with Drinfeld's functor `FormalOmega.Omega C₀ π` of Deligne data to the $B$-points of $f_0$, respectively of $g_0(\ell)$. The hypothesis `hCD` is the conjunction of: naturality of $\Theta$ and of each $\Theta T(\ell)$ in $B$; invariance of $\Theta$ under the twisted action of `awayUnits R₂ v₁` through $\iota_1$, and of $\Theta T(\ell)$ under the corresponding intersection with the $s_1$-conjugate; for algebraically closed $B = k$ with a fixed $\psi : Onr \to k$, surjectivity onto the $k$-points and the statement that two Deligne data have the same image precisely when they are related by the twisted action of such a $\gamma$, both for $\Theta$ and for each $\Theta T(\ell)$; the universal property expressing $f_0$ and each $g_0(\ell)$ as the quotient, in the category of such functors, of the uniformisation along these group actions (existence and uniqueness of the factoring transformation); compatibility of $\Theta T(\ell)$ with $d_0(\ell)$ and, via the twisted action of $s_1(\ell)$, with $d_1(\ell)$; and compatibility of $\Theta$ with $ar$ through the Frobenius twist `frobTwist Onr Fr 1` on the first coordinate and with $\overline{ar}$ through the twisted action of $\bar w_1(\mathrm{none})$.
--
--   Finally, the base change of $f_0$ and of each $g_0(\ell)$ along $\mathcal O_0 \to C_0 \to C$ is assumed to have integral source: the pullbacks $\mathcal X_C := \mathcal X_0 \times_{\mathcal O_0} C$ and $\mathcal Y_C(\ell) := \mathcal Y_0(\ell) \times_{\mathcal O_0} C$ are integral schemes (`hXCint`, `hYCint`).
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of a ring homomorphism $j : \bar F \to K(\mathcal X_C)$ into the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ such that the following seven statements hold. First, $j$ is $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-semilinear for the structural embedding: for every $z \in \overline{\mathbb Q}$, $j$ of the image of $z$ in $\bar F$ equals the image of $z \in C$ under `baseToFunctionField` of the second projection of $\mathcal X_C$. Second, the subfield of $K(\mathcal X_C)$ generated by the image of $C$ together with the image of $j$ is everything. Third, $j$ is injective in the strong linear-disjointness sense: for every finite subset $t \subseteq \bar F$ linearly independent over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and every family of coefficients $c : t \to C$, vanishing of $\sum_{x \in t} c(x) \cdot j(x)$ in $K(\mathcal X_C)$ forces $c \equiv 0$. Fourth, $j$ intertwines $W(1)$ with $ar$: for every endomorphism $a_{\mathcal X_C}$ of $\mathcal X_C$ whose composite with the first projection equals the first projection followed by $ar$ and which commutes with the second projection, and for every $x \in \bar F$, every open $U$ containing the generic point with $a_{\mathcal X_C}^{-1}U$ containing it as well, and every section $\mathrm{sec}$ over $U$ whose germ at the generic point is $j(x)$, the germ at the generic point of the pullback of $\mathrm{sec}$ along $a_{\mathcal X_C}$ is $j(W(1) \cdot x)$. Fifth, the same statement with $ar$ replaced by $\overline{ar}$ and $W(1)$ replaced by $W(0)$. Sixth, $j$ is Galois-equivariant in the corresponding sense: for $\tau$ in the decomposition subgroup of $A_1$ over $\mathbb Q$ and every endomorphism $t_C$ of $\mathcal X_C$ commuting with the first projection and inducing $dIso_1(\tau)$ on the second projection, the germ of the pullback of a section with germ $j(x)$ is $j(gal(\tau) \cdot x)$, where $\tau$ is regarded in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ through the inclusion of the decomposition subgroup. Seventh, for every prime $\ell \ne q, q'$ there is a ring homomorphism $j_\ell : \mathbb T.F(\ell) \to K(\mathcal Y_C(\ell))$ such that for each $k \in \{0, 1\}$ and each morphism $d_C : \mathcal Y_C(\ell) \to \mathcal X_C$ lying over $d_0(\ell)$ when $k = 0$ and over $d_1(\ell)$ when $k = 1$ and compatible with the projections to $\mathrm{Spec}\,C$, the pullback along $d_C$ of a section of $\mathcal X_C$ whose germ at the generic point is $j(x)$ has germ $j_\ell(\mathbb T.\varphi(\ell, k)(x))$ at the generic point of $\mathcal Y_C(\ell)$.
--
--   This is the transfer of the geometric Hecke-tower data on the Shimura curve attached to the indefinite quaternion algebra ramified exactly at $q$ and $q'$ to the Čerednik–Drinfeld integral model over the completion $C$ at a place above $q'$: the function field $\bar F$ of the characteristic-zero model is embedded, $C$-linearly disjointly and generating, into the function field of $\mathcal X_0 \otimes C$, compatibly with the two Atkin–Lehner involutions, the Galois action of the decomposition group, and the degeneracy maps of the tower. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.mumfordEmbedding_assembly_of_functionField_equiv_one_zero_of_two_mul_dvd`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.mumfordEmbedding_assembly_of_functionField_equiv_one_zero_of_two_mul_dvd) in the construction of the Mumford-curve description of the special fibre.
